<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagyu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kagyu school</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagyu is one of the main schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, tracing its teaching lineage from the Indian masters Tilopa and Naropa through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> Marpa, Milarepa and Gampopa in the 11th and 12th centuries. Gampopa's disciples founded several sub-schools, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu">Drikung Kagyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa Kagyu</a> and Phagdru Kagyu, the last linked to the Phagmodrupa rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagyu.jpg" alt="Kagyu" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahasiddha_Naropa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Kagyu, in Tibetan bka' brgyud, means Oral Lineage or Whispered Transmission, and it is one of the four major schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. Its lineages begin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> with Marpa Lotsawa (1012–1097), a householder who trained as a translator and travelled three times to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and four times to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> in search of teachings. His principal teachers were the siddhas Nāropa, from whom he received the close lineage of mahāmudrā and tantric teachings, and Maitrīpāda, from whom he received the distant lineage. Nāropa had been the chief disciple of Tilopa, who had received Four Lineages of Instructions, and Nāropa codified them as the Six Dharmas of Naropa.</p><p>Marpa gave his main lineage to Milarepa (1040–1123), and Milarepa's student Gampopa (1079–1153) became the central figure. Marpa, Milarepa and Gampopa are together considered the founders of the school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The main teachings of the Kagyu are Mahamudra and the Six Dharmas of Naropa. Gampopa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadam-school">Kadampa</a> monk, combined the monastic discipline and stages-of-the-path teaching of the Kadam order with the teachings he had received from Milarepa. He founded Daklha Gampo Monastery and established a celibate monastic order, in contrast to Marpa and Milarepa, whose tradition consisted mainly of householders and hermits practising in solitude. This lineage became known as Dagpo Kagyu, and most major Kagyu lineages can be traced through Gampopa.</p><p>Later writers spoke of Four Major and Eight Minor lineages of the Dagpo Kagyu. The four major branches were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a>, founded by Düsum Khyenpa, 1st Karmapa Lama (1110–1193), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barom-kagyu">Barom Kagyu</a>, the Tshalpa Kagyu and the Phagdru Kagyu, founded by Phagmo Drupa Dorje Gyalpo (1110–1170). The eight minor ones derive from Phagmo Drupa's disciples and include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu">Drikung Kagyu</a>, from Drigung Monastery, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa lineage</a>. These terms date only from writings of the 19th century, and they do not measure size: the Drikung school was probably the largest and most influential in the 13th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The independent schools surviving today are chiefly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu">Drikung Kagyu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa lineage</a>; the Karma Kagyu is the largest and is headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapa</a>. The Phagmodrupa declined between 1435 and 1481 and were eclipsed by the Rinpungpa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>, who supported the Karma Kagyu. In the 15th century the Tshalpa Kagyu was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a>. Marpa's transmission of the Guhyasamāja Tantra, through Tshurton Wangi Dorje, passed in time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>, founder of the Gelug. In the 19th century Jamgon Kongtrul gathered the surviving initiations of Marpa's teachings in the Kagyu Ngak Dzö. After the death of the 16th Karmapa in 1981, his followers disagreed over the identity of his successor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kagyu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Changchub Gyaltsen, myriarch of Phagmodru, a domain linked to the <b>Kagyu</b> monastery of Densatil, fought the Sakya regime's administrators from the 1340s.</blockquote><a href="/state/phagmodrupa-dynasty">Phagmodrupa dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
